```java
import java.math.BigInteger;
import java.math.RoundingMode;
import java.util.Locale;

public class PowerOfThree {
    public static boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) {
            return false;
        }

        // Convert n to a BigInteger to avoid integer overflow
        BigInteger bigN = BigInteger.valueOf(n);
        int exponent = bigN.log(3).intValueExact();
        return bigN.pow(exponent).compareTo(BigInteger.valueOf(n)) == 0;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 27;
        boolean result = isPowerOfThree(n);
        System.out.println(result); // true

        n = 0;
        result = isPowerOfThree(n);
        System.out.println(result); // false

        n = -1;
        result = isPowerOfThree(n);
        System.out.println(result); // false
    }
}
```

This solution first checks if `n` is positive (since `3^x` is always positive for integer `x`). Then, it converts `n` to a `BigInteger` to avoid integer overflow and calculates the logarithm base 3 using the `log()` method. This returns a `BigDecimal` value, so we use `intValueExact()` to get the integer value of the exponent. Finally, we check if `n` is equal to `3` raised to the calculated exponent using the `pow()` method of `BigInteger`.

Note that this solution assumes that `int` values are within the range `-231` to `231 - 1`, as specified in the problem statement. If you need to handle larger values, you may need to use a different approach.

